1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,502
Before you pay another tuition bill for a computer science degree,

2
00:00:03,502 --> 00:00:07,005
look at where it ranks among 73 majors for getting that first job.

3
00:00:07,125 --> 00:00:09,029
For twenty years the advice was simple.

4
00:00:09,029 --> 00:00:11,323
Learn to code, and you'll never be out of work.

5
00:00:11,673 --> 00:00:16,691
The New York Fed tracks recent college graduates, aged 22 to 27, major by major.

6
00:00:16,691 --> 00:00:18,071
In its latest numbers,

7
00:00:18,071 --> 00:00:23,026
computer engineering has the second-highest unemployment rate of all 73 majors.

8
00:00:23,026 --> 00:00:24,719
Computer science is fourth.

9
00:00:24,839 --> 00:00:29,281
That puts a new computer science grad, at 7 percent unemployed,

10
00:00:29,281 --> 00:00:34,570
behind art history at 6.7, philosophy at 5.1, and nutrition science at 4.5.

11
00:00:34,690 --> 00:00:36,685
So is computer science still worth it?

12
00:00:36,685 --> 00:00:39,100
The answer is more interesting than yes or no.

13
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:43,126
The first thing to notice is how narrow this is.

14
00:00:43,126 --> 00:00:45,245
Not every hard major has this problem.

15
00:00:45,245 --> 00:00:48,089
Electrical engineering grads, on the same campuses,

16
00:00:48,089 --> 00:00:51,714
doing much of the same math, had a 3.2 percent unemployment rate.

17
00:00:51,714 --> 00:00:54,390
Mechanical engineering, four point four percent.

18
00:00:54,510 --> 00:00:57,172
The overall rate for all recent grads was 4.2.

19
00:00:57,172 --> 00:01:00,182
So the trouble isn't engineering, and it isn't math.

20
00:01:00,182 --> 00:01:04,117
It's concentrated in the majors that feed entry-level software jobs.

21
00:01:04,237 --> 00:01:06,932
Other number-heavy majors are spread out.

22
00:01:06,932 --> 00:01:11,927
Physics sits at 6.6 percent, information systems at 6.0, mathematics at 5.8.

23
00:01:11,927 --> 00:01:14,951
But finance, just as heavy on math, is at 2.8.

24
00:01:14,951 --> 00:01:17,974
Nursing, a demanding science major, is at 2.1.

25
00:01:18,094 --> 00:01:20,292
So hard math doesn't predict the trouble.

26
00:01:20,292 --> 00:01:22,329
Finance and nursing are proof of that.

27
00:01:22,329 --> 00:01:25,545
What sets computer science and computer engineering apart is

28
00:01:25,545 --> 00:01:29,029
where they usually lead: straight into entry-level software jobs.

29
00:01:29,149 --> 00:01:30,364
One caution up front.

30
00:01:30,364 --> 00:01:34,586
These figures are for 2024, one year, and some majors have small samples.

31
00:01:34,586 --> 00:01:36,494
Treat the exact ranking as rough.

32
00:01:36,494 --> 00:01:40,427
The gap between software and the rest is the part to take seriously.

33
00:01:40,777 --> 00:01:42,674
Why would software be different?

34
00:01:42,674 --> 00:01:45,402
A Stanford team led by Erik Brynjolfsson looks

35
00:01:45,402 --> 00:01:48,070
at payroll records for workers aged 22 to 25.

36
00:01:48,070 --> 00:01:52,399
In the occupations most exposed to AI, which include software developers,

37
00:01:52,399 --> 00:01:56,550
employment for that age group was about 19 percent lower by June 2026,

38
00:01:56,550 --> 00:01:59,337
relative to young workers in less exposed jobs.

39
00:01:59,337 --> 00:02:01,590
A year earlier the gap was 15 percent.

40
00:02:01,710 --> 00:02:05,692
The same study finds the drop comes from fewer hires, not more layoffs,

41
00:02:05,692 --> 00:02:09,787
and it finds no evidence of widespread job loss across the whole economy.

42
00:02:09,907 --> 00:02:13,956
That fits what the New York Fed table shows: the first job is harder to get.

43
00:02:13,956 --> 00:02:17,419
But the authors call their findings patterns, not proof of cause.

44
00:02:17,419 --> 00:02:20,616
Remote work and interest rates were moving at the same time,

45
00:02:20,616 --> 00:02:22,321
as we covered in our last video.

46
00:02:22,671 --> 00:02:24,831
Here is the part that usually gets left out.

47
00:02:24,831 --> 00:02:28,168
Look at what happens to the computer science grads who do get hired.

48
00:02:28,288 --> 00:02:32,188
Underemployment means working a job that doesn't need a degree.

49
00:02:32,188 --> 00:02:34,541
For all recent grads, it's 39 percent.

50
00:02:34,541 --> 00:02:35,779
For art history, 45.

51
00:02:35,779 --> 00:02:36,955
For philosophy, 47.

52
00:02:36,955 --> 00:02:38,503
For computer science, 19.

53
00:02:38,503 --> 00:02:40,298
For computer engineering, 16.

54
00:02:40,418 --> 00:02:41,210
And the pay.

55
00:02:41,210 --> 00:02:46,027
The typical early-career computer science grad earns 87 thousand dollars.

56
00:02:46,027 --> 00:02:48,271
Computer engineering, 90 thousand.

57
00:02:48,271 --> 00:02:49,261
All majors, 58.

58
00:02:49,261 --> 00:02:50,317
Art history, 45.

59
00:02:50,317 --> 00:02:55,464
By mid-career, computer science is at 120 thousand, against 87 for all majors.

60
00:02:55,584 --> 00:02:58,321
So computer science didn't turn into a bad major.

61
00:02:58,321 --> 00:03:00,778
It turned into a high-risk, high-reward one.

62
00:03:00,778 --> 00:03:02,565
The first job is harder to land.

63
00:03:02,565 --> 00:03:04,129
The jobs that land pay well.

64
00:03:04,479 --> 00:03:07,683
The government's long-range outlook is more optimistic.

65
00:03:07,683 --> 00:03:10,188
The Bureau of Labor Statistics counts about

66
00:03:10,188 --> 00:03:12,576
1.9 million jobs for software developers,

67
00:03:12,576 --> 00:03:17,295
testers and quality analysts, with a median salary of about 134 thousand dollars.

68
00:03:17,295 --> 00:03:21,082
It projects those jobs will grow 10 percent over the next decade,

69
00:03:21,082 --> 00:03:22,538
much faster than average.

70
00:03:22,658 --> 00:03:24,504
That's a projection, not a promise.

71
00:03:24,504 --> 00:03:27,247
But it separates two questions parents often mix up.

72
00:03:27,247 --> 00:03:28,618
Is the career still there?

73
00:03:28,618 --> 00:03:29,778
The forecast says yes.

74
00:03:29,778 --> 00:03:31,941
Is the first job harder to get right now?

75
00:03:31,941 --> 00:03:33,101
The data says yes too.

76
00:03:33,451 --> 00:03:37,870
You could argue none of this matters, because a degree still beats no degree.

77
00:03:37,870 --> 00:03:38,559
That's true.

78
00:03:38,559 --> 00:03:43,150
Young workers without a degree had a 7.4 percent unemployment rate in September,

79
00:03:43,150 --> 00:03:44,929
worse than recent grads at 5.2.

80
00:03:44,929 --> 00:03:48,258
And grads earned about 60 thousand dollars a year in 2025,

81
00:03:48,258 --> 00:03:51,013
against 40 thousand for young high school grads.

82
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:53,947
But that comparison is about whether to go to college.

83
00:03:53,947 --> 00:03:56,864
The question here is which major, and for the first job,

84
00:03:56,864 --> 00:03:59,782
the major now matters more than the old advice admitted.

85
00:04:00,132 --> 00:04:03,043
Put the two numbers on one chart and computer science

86
00:04:03,043 --> 00:04:05,735
sits in a corner by itself: near the top for pay,

87
00:04:05,735 --> 00:04:06,944
near the top for risk.

88
00:04:06,944 --> 00:04:11,503
A few majors, like nursing and electrical engineering, pay well with far less risk.

89
00:04:11,853 --> 00:04:14,184
What the data can say is narrow: in 2024,

90
00:04:14,184 --> 00:04:18,676
the first job out of a software degree was harder to get than almost any other,

91
00:04:18,676 --> 00:04:21,007
and it paid better than almost any other.

92
00:04:21,007 --> 00:04:25,044
What it can't say yet is whether AI is the reason, or whether it lasts.

93
00:04:25,164 --> 00:04:27,015
So if your kid is in computer science,

94
00:04:27,015 --> 00:04:29,305
the useful question isn't "should they switch?"

95
00:04:29,305 --> 00:04:31,400
It's "what's their plan for the first job?"
